language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ bowls_and_oranges: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ There are bowls, numbered from 1, placed in a line at exact intervals of 1 metre, and a number of oranges. Place all the oranges in the bowls, with no more than one orange in each bowl, so that there are no three oranges A, B and C such that the distance between A and B is equal to the distance between B and C. The bowls holding oranges are given in ascending order.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `bowls_and_oranges.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/bowls_and_oranges.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `bowls_and_oranges_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py bowls_and_oranges.json
$   savilerow model.eprime bowls_and_oranges_1.param -run-solver

$ n: the number of bowls, numbered 1 to n
given n : int
$ m: the number of oranges
given m : int

$ x[i]: the bowl holding the i-th orange in ascending order, indices 1-based
find x : matrix indexed by [int(1..m)] of int(1..n)

branching on [x]
such that
$ the oranges are in distinct bowls, listed in ascending order
forAll i : int(1..m-1) . x[i] < x[i+1],
$ no three oranges i < j < k (in bowl order) are equally spaced: the distance from i to j differs from the distance from j to k
forAll i : int(1..m) . forAll j : int(1..m) . forAll k : int(1..m) . ((i < j /\ j < k) -> x[i] + x[k] != 2 * x[j])
